  "query_preview": "..\n\n-  Colder temperatures, with areas of accumulating snow today\n   into Saturday.\n\n-  Snow showers Sunday, bringing minor snow accumulations.\n\n-  Warming trend by the middle of next week.\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning depicts a low pressure \ncirculation over northern Nevada.  This system is sending \nmoisture north into the Northern Rockies, bringing light \nprecipitation to north-central Idaho.  Precipitation there \nwill fall off this morning.  Also visible on satellite is our \nnext weather system.  It is currently spinning over central \nAlberta, moving south just east of the Continental Divide. This \nsystem, combined with favorable northwest flow over the Northern \nRockies, will be a good pattern for snow showers along and east \nof US Highway 93 in western  Montana.  This area will have the \nhighest probability (60-90 percent) for accumulating snow \n(0.10 inches or more), primarily this afternoon through Saturday \nmorning. Those traveling across mountain passes along the Divide, \nsuch as Marias, Homestake, and Marias passes should prepare for \nwinter driving conditions given colder temperatures and periods \nof accumulating snowfall. The highest snow accumulations will \nfocus across the higher elevations of Glacier Park, the Swan and \nMission Mountains, and the Bob Marshall Wilderness, where \nnorthwest flow will trigger numerous orographically driven snow \nshowers. Finally, model soundings and timeheights suggest a \nfavorable set up for isolated snow bands forming downstream of the\nBitterroot Crest along the ID/MT border. These bands will be \nisolated areas of poor visibility and localized snow accumulations\nalong the US-93 and I-90 corridors south and east of Missoula \nSaturday morning.\n\nAn active pattern will continue Sunday into early next week,\nwith colder temperatures holding snow levels near valley floors.\nThe next Pacific trough moves along the Oregon and California\ncoast by Sunday, with southwest flow aloft spreading moisture\ninland across the Northern Rockies. Model guidance suggests snow\nlevels around 2,000 feet Sunday morning, with snow showers leading\nto minor snow accumulations across most elevations and areas\nthrough Sunday morning. Snow levels will climb near 3,000 feet\nalong and south of the I-90 corridor by Sunday afternoon, with\nsnow levels remaining down to valley floors in northwest Montana\nthrough the day. This system does not appear to be a major snow\nproducer, with most locations seeing a trace to 1 inch of snow,\nwith mountain areas along the ID/MT border seeing a couple inches.\n\nA warming trend arrives by the middle of next week as a trough\ndeepens off the West Coast and a ridge of high pressure amplifies\nacross the Northern Rockies. Model disagreement increased by next\nWednesday, highlighted by various solutions within ensemble\ncluster analysis fields. Half of cluster members suggest\nincreasing moisture and precipitation associated with an\natmospheric river pushing on the west coast within southwest flow,\nwith the half of cluster members keeping the Northern Rockies\ndry.",
